      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of women who attended the party. We know that each man danced with exactly three women and each woman danced with exactly two men. We also know that there were twelve men at the party.\n\nLet's denote the number of women as \\( w \\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of \"man-woman\" interactions from the men's perspective. Since each man danced with three women, the total number of man-woman interactions is:\n\\[ 12 \\text{ men} \\times 3 \\text{ women per man} = 36 \\text{ interactions} \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the total number of \"man-woman\" interactions from the women's perspective. Since each woman danced with two men, the total number of man-woman interactions is also:\n\\[ w \\text{ women} \\times 2 \\text{ men per woman} = 2w \\text{ interactions} \\]\n\nSince the total number of man-woman interactions is the same from both perspectives, we can set the two expressions equal to each other:\n\\[ 36 = 2w \\]\n\nTo find \\( w \\), we solve the equation:\n\\[ w = \\frac{36}{2} = 18 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of women who attended the party is:\n\\[ \\boxed{18} \\]",
